{0 vcard}

vCard 4.0 for OCaml. An implementation of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6350.html}RFC 6350} with the parameter
escapes of {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6868.html}RFC 6868} and the
extensions of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9554.html}RFC 9554} and
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9555.html}RFC 9555} that JSContact
conversion needs.

{1 The entry point}

{!Vcard} is the single module of the library. A {!Vcard.t} is a list of
{!Vcard.Property.t}, each an unfolded content line with its group, name,
parameters and wire value.

- {!Vcard.Property} reads a value as the data type the caller asks for and
  exposes the common parameters.
- {!Vcard.N} and {!Vcard.Adr} view the structured values of the [N] and [ADR]
  properties, with the components
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9554.html} RFC 9554} adds.
- {!Vcard.Date} holds the date and time value types, including the reduced and
  truncated forms such as [--0412].
- {!Vcard.Jscomps} reads the [JSCOMPS] parameter of
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9555.html} RFC 9555}.
- {!Vcard.Registry} lists the registered properties with their default value
  types and cardinalities.

{1 Lossless by design}

The reader keeps every property, known or unknown, with its parameters and its
wire value, and the writer puts them back. A card read and written again holds
the same properties. Property and parameter names are uppercased and parameter
values are re-quoted, so the text may differ where the input was lenient.

{1 Shape versus meaning}

{!Vcard.of_string} enforces the shape of a card, which is the [BEGIN], [VERSION]
and [END] framing and the content line syntax. {!Vcard.validate} holds a card to
the rules {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6350.html} RFC 6350} states in
prose, such as the cardinality of [N] or the range of [PREF].

{1 Reading and writing}

{[
  match Vcard.of_string s with
  | Ok cards -> List.iter (fun c -> print_string (Vcard.to_string c)) cards
  | Error msg -> prerr_endline msg
]}
